Chapter 16
Midnight had settled over the streets near the station. Curtis walked with the duffel on one shoulder, compression clothes under an open jacket, braids loose. He was halfway to the net café when he caught a flash of familiar hair under a streetlight and slowed.
Hina.
She stood a block ahead, flyer bag clutched tight, backed against a closed storefront. Two men in cheap suits had her boxed in. One of them laughed, reaching for her wrist.
“Come on, just one drink. You were handing those out earlier—”
“Leave me alone!” Hina’s voice cracked sharp. “I’m not on the clock!”
Curtis’s pace changed. The low, steady pull in his chest stayed quiet, but his eyes locked on the pair. He crossed the distance in long, even strides, boots quiet on the pavement, and stopped just behind the closer man.
“She said leave her alone.”
The two turned. They had to look up. Curtis didn’t raise his voice. He didn’t need to. The streetlight caught the heterochromia and the fresh bruise still fading along his hairline. Hina’s eyes went wide when she saw him.
The man who’d grabbed for her wrist let go. Neither of them spoke right away. Curtis waited, expression even, while the midnight quiet stretched around the four of them.
The closer man lifted both hands, backing up a step. “Hey, we were just talking. No problem.”
“Then keep walking,” Curtis said. Even. Flat.
They did. The second one muttered something under his breath but didn’t test it. Their footsteps faded down the block. The street went quiet again.
Hina stayed against the shutter for a second, bag clutched tight, then pushed off it. Her cheeks were flushed—fear first, then the other warmth that always showed up when he was close. “Curtis. You’re—you should be in the infirmary. I saw the picture.”
“Left early.” He glanced down the street the men had taken, then back at her. “You good?”
“Yeah. Just… they followed me from the station.” She adjusted the bag on her shoulder, eyes flicking over the bruise at his hairline. “You didn’t have to step in.”
“Didn’t like the way they were talking.” He shifted the duffel. “You heading home or to the café?”
“Home. Train’s still running.” She hesitated, then added, softer, “You can walk me to the platform. If you’re already out.”
“Works for me.”
They started down the sidewalk together. Hina stayed a half-step closer than she needed to. The low, steady pull in his chest remained quiet while the midnight city moved around them and the last of the adrenaline faded from her shoulders.
Footsteps rushed them from behind.
One of the men had doubled back. The knife caught the streetlight as he lunged. Curtis turned and stopped it with his open palm. The blade punched clean through his hand, point exiting between the knuckles. Blood welled fast.
He didn’t flinch. His fingers closed around the flat of the knife and snapped it. The broken piece clattered to the pavement. The man froze, eyes locked on the wound that wasn’t slowing Curtis down.
Curtis looked at him, heterochromia steady, voice low.
“I had a rough day. If you want to play villain right now, then I will deal with you how I dealt with the others.”
The second man had already backed onto the curb. The one with the broken knife stared at the blood running down Curtis’s wrist, then at his face, and decided the rest of the sentence wasn’t worth hearing. He turned and ran. His friend followed.
Hina’s breath had caught hard. She stared at the hole in Curtis’s palm, then at him. “Your hand—”
“It’s fine.” He flexed the fingers once. The bleeding was already slowing. “Come on. Platform’s this way.”
He started walking again. Hina fell in beside him, closer than before, eyes still flicking to the wounded hand while the two men disappeared into the midnight streets behind them.
Hina didn’t ask. She just grabbed his good wrist at the station entrance and pulled him onto the opposite platform, then onto a different train, then up three flights of stairs to a narrow apartment door. She unlocked it fast and tugged him inside.
The lights were on. A girl about their age stepped out of the kitchen in an oversized shirt, chopsticks in one hand, and stopped dead.
“Hina?! Hey—that’s your coworker. And the same kid from the news.”
Curtis lifted his uninjured hand in a short wave, the other still loosely wrapped in a takeaway napkin Hina had stolen from a stand. “Hey. Sorry for dropping in.”
The roommate’s eyes went wide, then flicked to the bloodied napkin, then back to his face. Color rose in her cheeks. “You’re—you’re taller in person. And bleeding. On our floor.”
“Working on that,” Curtis said.
Hina kicked her shoes off and pointed him toward the couch. “Sit. I’ll get the kit. Yui, this is Curtis. Curtis, Yui. She’s not going to post the picture if she wants to keep living here.”
Yui raised both hands, still staring. “Wasn’t gonna. But the group chat is going to lose it when I tell them the USJ guy is on our couch.”
Curtis sat. The low, steady pull in his chest stayed quiet while Hina disappeared into the bathroom and Yui hovered in the kitchen doorway, trying not to look like she was looking.
Hina came back with the kit and knelt in front of the couch, already unwrapping the napkin. “What were you even doing out this late? Recovery Girl can’t have cleared you.”
“Didn’t want to stay in the hospital,” Curtis said. He watched her clean the wound, expression even. “Was looking for a net café before I ran into you. Same as the other nights.”
Yui leaned on the kitchen counter, chopsticks forgotten. “A basement suite just freed up in this building. Landlord posted it this morning. Cheap. Has a real door and a bathroom. Way better than a café booth.”
Hina’s hands paused on the bandage. She looked up at Curtis, eyes hopeful, cheeks warm. She didn’t say anything. She just waited, the question sitting right there between them.
Curtis glanced from her to Yui, then back. The low, steady pull in his chest stayed quiet. “How cheap?”
Yui grinned. “Cheap enough that two part-time jobs could cover it. I can text you the listing if you want.”
Hina went back to wrapping his hand, but the small smile stayed. She didn’t push. She didn’t have to. Curtis flexed the bandaged fingers once and nodded.
“Send it.”
Curtis stood and slung the duffel over his good shoulder. The bandage on his hand held. “I should head out. Let you two sleep.”
Hina rose with him, closer than the small living room required. “You can stay.”
The words came out quicker than she meant. Color sat high on her cheeks. Behind her, Yui had gone quiet in the kitchen doorway, arms crossed, the same warmth visible in her face every time she looked at him. Free Love worked through the apartment the way it always did—low, constant, heavier in the confined space. Neither girl moved to give him room.
Curtis paused, strap creaking. He looked between them, expression even. “You sure? This wasn’t planned. It’s impolite to drop in and then take the couch.”
“We don’t mind,” Yui said. Her voice was lighter than usual. She cleared her throat. “Couch is free. Or the floor. Whatever. Just… don’t bleed on the cushions.”
Hina nodded, still close. “Stay. At least until morning. The trains thin out after midnight anyway.”
Curtis studied them both for a second longer, then let the duffel slide back down. “Alright. Thanks.”
He set the bag by the couch. The low, steady pull in his chest remained quiet and unnamed while the two girls exchanged a glance they didn’t explain and the apartment settled around the fact that he wasn’t leaving.
Curtis took the couch. He stretched out in the compression clothes, bandaged hand resting on his stomach, and was asleep before the apartment fully quieted. The low, steady pull in his chest stayed exactly where it always was.
In Hina’s room the lights were off. She lay on her side, face pressed into the pillow, thighs pressed tight together. Free Love didn’t care that he was unconscious on the other side of the wall. Her thoughts wouldn’t stay clean.
He’s right there. On our couch. That body under those clothes. I keep seeing him grab that guy’s knife like it was nothing. Her hand slid under the waistband of her shorts before she could stop it. I want him to pin me down and fuck me until I can’t think. Want those hands on my hips while he talks in that flat voice like he’s just asking about train times. God, I’m so wet already.
Across the hall, Yui stared at the ceiling, one knee bent, breathing uneven. The same heat sat low in her stomach.
Hina’s coworker. The one from the news. And he’s sleeping ten feet from my door. She bit her lip. Bet he doesn’t even know what he does to people. Bet I could climb on top of him on that couch and he’d just blink those weird eyes and ask if I was comfortable. I want his cock in me. Want to feel how deep he goes while he still thinks this is just a polite overnight.
Neither of them got up. The apartment stayed quiet except for the soft sound of Curtis breathing on the couch and the restless shifting behind two closed doors. The want stayed thick in both rooms, unspoken, while he slept through all of it.
Sleep didn’t stay clean.
Curtis stood in the USJ plaza again. Aizawa was on the ground, blood running from his hairline, one eye swollen shut, the capture weapon slack. Nomu’s massive hand pinned him. The image wouldn’t move. Then the plaza cracked and became older woods—Appalachian dark, something with too many joints dragging a body he hadn’t been fast enough to reach. A girl’s voice cut off mid-scream. His own hands, younger, covered in someone else’s blood. Failure stacked on failure. The mask wouldn’t come off this time.
He jerked awake at 4 a.m.
The apartment was dark. Couch springs creaked under him. His bandaged hand had clenched so hard the wrap had split. Heart hammering. The nightmare clung—Aizawa’s blood, the woods, the things he hadn’t stopped. He sat up slow, elbows on his knees, and breathed until the shaking eased. Heterochromia caught the faint streetlight through the blinds.
No one else was up. Hina’s and Yui’s doors stayed closed. Curtis rubbed his face, stood, and moved quietly to the kitchen for water. The low, steady pull in his chest was quiet again. The nightmares weren’t. He drank, set the glass down, and stayed on his feet in the dark, waiting for the clock to decide the night was actually over.
